al I would call him to congratulate  him and whenever we would win a deal he  would call me to congratulate me now by  the way we were tough competitors why  would we do that  because we both understood that we were  building a larger market so you can  understand our our goal as philanthropic  you can understand it as making the  world a better place but you can also  understand it as self-interested in the  building more people who understand the  concepts of network computing and  platforms and so forth benefits everyone  including Google 20% time was a rule  with vetted again when I started there  there were two actually interesting one  was don't be evil in the second one was  20% time don't be evil I thought was a  joke  um because I mean nobody here is evil  nor the evil you want to be evil and so  I was sitting in the original conference  room in the small building when we've  started and there's this conversation  about ad targeting and a particular ad  click and one of the engineers his name  is Ron pounds the table had said that  would be evil and I'm like oh okay what  just happened and the whole mood of the  room changes and there's this huge  debate over whether that's evil or not  and ultimately they didn't do the change  and so I thought what's the analogy  they're in it the analogy was it was  it's the Kanban system right in Japan  the notion that any employee can stop  the line if they see a poor-quality  thing and it works because of shared  values strong shared values a strong  buy-in to principles takes you far I  think that helped us and that I think it  may or may not be true today that the  company has that feeling but there  certainly its history is clearly that  thank you guys so much for watching I  made this video because Sam Boorstin  asked me to so there's a famous  entrepreneur that you are made of  profile next leave it down in the  comments below and I'll see what I can  do I'd also love to know which of the  ten rules had the bigges